WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
How many equal square tiles would cover a small floor?

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.520
A long piece of string cannot cover the surface.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:12.002
We need a different measure from perimeter: area tells us how much flat space a shape covers.

00:00:12.002 --> 00:00:16.264
Our learning goal is to explain and find area using square units.

00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:17.824
Success criteria.

00:00:17.824 --> 00:00:21.144
I can cover a surface without gaps or overlaps.

00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:24.144
I can use rows and columns to count squares.

00:00:24.144 --> 00:00:26.731
I can distinguish area from perimeter.

00:00:26.731 --> 00:00:30.411
Arrange equal unit squares without gaps or overlaps.

00:00:30.411 --> 00:00:32.731
Each square covers one square unit.

00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:32.891
For a rectangle, equal rows let us count efficiently with multiplication.

00:00:32.891 --> 00:00:43.270
A square centimetre is a square one centimetre along each side, not a one-centimetre length.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.909
A rectangle has eight columns and five rows of square-centimetre tiles.

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:50.549
Predict how many unit squares cover it.

00:00:50.549 --> 00:00:54.348
Each row contains eight squares, and there are five equal rows.

00:00:54.348 --> 00:00:57.227
Five groups of eight make forty unit squares.

00:00:57.227 --> 00:01:00.067
The area is forty square centimetres.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:06.736
Multiplying eight centimetres by five centimetres counts the same tiled surface, so we write the squared unit.

00:01:06.736 --> 00:01:09.816
A play mat is seven metres by four metres.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:12.576
Imagine covering it with one-metre squares.

00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:16.416
There are seven squares along each row, with four rows altogether.

00:01:16.416 --> 00:01:18.637
What would the surface measurement be?

00:01:18.637 --> 00:01:21.357
The area is twenty-eight square metres.

00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:27.277
The perimeter is a different quantity: seven plus four plus seven plus four is twenty-two metres.

00:01:27.277 --> 00:01:30.385
Area counts the cover; perimeter measures the border.

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:35.465
Adding eight and five does not count the tiles covering an eight-by-five rectangle.

00:01:35.465 --> 00:01:37.745
It counts only two side lengths.

00:01:37.745 --> 00:01:43.293
To find area, use all five rows of eight equal squares, with no gaps or overlaps.

00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:47.253
A rectangle has six columns and four rows of unit squares.

00:01:47.253 --> 00:01:48.653
What is its area?

00:01:48.653 --> 00:01:51.947
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:55.067
Four groups of six make twenty-four square units.

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:58.907
You can check by counting the tiles or adding six four times.

00:01:58.907 --> 00:02:01.121
No tile should be counted twice.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:04.361
Area counts equal squares covering a surface.

00:02:04.361 --> 00:02:08.961
Use rows and columns to count efficiently and always label the square unit.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.721
Draw two rectangles with the same area on squared paper.

00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:15.201
Do they have to have the same perimeter?

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.721
Explain with a drawing.

00:02:16.721 --> 00:02:19.276
Reflect on which model helped you and why.
